{
  "info": {
    "_postman_id": "a1b2c3d4-5e6f-4a7b-8c9d-0e1f2a3b4c5d",
    "name": "LedgerOne - User Management",
    "description": "User Management module API collection (Create, Invite, Get, Update, Activate, Suspend, Deactivate, List, Search). Reflects the implementation exactly as shipped in apps/api/src/shared/user-management.\n\nDesign note (self-contained, no Organization dependency): `users.tenant_id`/`users.company_uuid` carry no DB-level foreign key to `tenants`/`companies` (06_DATABASE_STANDARDS.md FK-002) and the Business layer never validates them against Organization's real data (documented in create-user.service.ts) \u2014 so this collection uses a fixed test {{tenantId}} and {{companyUuid}} rather than chaining a real Tenant/Company through the Organization collection. This sidesteps a genuine architectural gap: User Management's `X-Tenant-Id` header carries the tenant's *numeric* internal id (tenant-id-header.schema.ts), which no HTTP endpoint anywhere in this system ever exposes (06_DATABASE_STANDARDS.md PK-003) \u2014 there is no way to obtain it from a freshly-created Tenant's uuid without direct database access. Mirrors Authentication's own collection, which hardcodes {{tenantId}}=1 in its environment for the identical reason.\n\nUses Postman's {{$randomEmail}} dynamic variable on both create-style requests so the collection can be run repeatedly against the same database without ever colliding on Ch.10.8's per-tenant email-uniqueness rule \u2014 no manual edits required between runs.\n\nKnown gap: List/Search are unpaginated (07_REST_API_STANDARDS.md Ch.14 otherwise mandates cursor pagination) \u2014 flagged in list-users.controller.ts/search-users.controller.ts as a Repository-layer limitation, not fixed by this collection.\n\nRun order for a full pass, top to bottom: every request chains off collection variables set by 'Create User - Success' and 'Invite User - Success' ({{userUuid}}/{{userEmail}}, {{secondUserUuid}}/{{secondUserEmail}}) \u2014 run in order, do not run negative-path requests out of order relative to the lifecycle sequence (Activate -> Suspend -> Activate (reinstate) -> Deactivate).",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
  "auth": {
    "type": "noauth"
  },
